    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe membership-level storage itself fits the codebase well: a `membership_level` column plus a create-time `computeMembershipLevel`/`effectiveMembershipLevel` pair in `ClinicServiceImpl` mirrors the existing `countNamesakes` / `countHouseholdMembers` pattern for deterministic, create-time-computed stored fields, and `formatMembershipLevel` in `OwnerMapper` preferring the stored value is a natural read-side addition. The schema change is applied consistently across all four dialects. But two things keep this from reading as a coherent, additive change:\n\n1. **The change bundles in the removal of an unrelated business invariant.** The diff deletes `assertHouseholdIsUnique` and the `if (!sharesHousehold) assertHouseholdIsUnique(owner)` guard in `saveOwner`, relaxing the household-uniqueness rule so that undeclared duplicate household members are now *created and flagged* rather than *rejected*. That is a behavioral/scope change, not the additive membership-level feature the spec asks for. The spec is only about capping `membershipLevel`.\n\n   The javadoc justifies the removal (\"Allowing household members to coexist is what lets the household membership-level ceiling compare a new owner against the existing members\"), but this rationale is overstated: declared household members (`sharesHousehold == true`) already coexisted under the old code, so `computeMembershipLevel` already has existing members to compare against without dropping the uniqueness check. The ceiling feature does not require deleting `assertHouseholdIsUnique`. A maintainer would be surprised to find a feature PR silently dropping a duplicate-rejection rule and rewriting the `saveOwner` contract javadoc. Either keep the uniqueness check (the feature works within the existing declared-member set), or, if relaxing it is genuinely intended, split that into its own change with its own justification rather than folding it into this one.\n\n2. **Minor: the \"stored-else-recompute-from-points\" rule is now duplicated** between `ClinicServiceImpl.effectiveMembershipLevel` and `OwnerMapper.formatMembershipLevel`. The two live in different layers so some divergence is acceptable, but they encode the same resolution logic and will need to stay in lockstep; worth a note, and ideally the canonical resolution should have a single home that both layers defer to.\n\nFinding 1 is the actionable blocker; finding 2 is a smaller coherence nit.",
